# Installation

This page adds Kiln to an existing Rails 8.1 app and runs a first build. The current release is <%= Kiln::VERSION %>.

## Add the gem

```ruby
# Gemfile
gem "kiln"
```

Run `bundle install`. Kiln adds its rake tasks through a Railtie, so there is no generator to run.

## Write config/kiln.rb

Kiln loads `config/kiln.rb` before the app's initializers. It holds two blocks: `Kiln.configure` for options and `Kiln.pages` for the routes that need params.

```ruby
# config/kiln.rb
Kiln.configure do |config|
  config.url = "https://example.com"
  config.not_found = "/404"
end

Kiln.pages do
  params :post, -> { Post.published.map { { slug: it.slug } } }
  skip :search, reason: "dynamic"
end
```

`url` is the public base URL. Its host is sent with every request, and `sitemap.xml` is written only when it is set. `not_found` is the path rendered to `404.html`. An app whose routes all have no params can leave `Kiln.pages` out. Param sources are lambdas, so they can name models that do not exist yet when the file loads.

## Run a build

```sh
RAILS_ENV=production bin/rails kiln:build
```

The build runs in the environment Rails boots in. `kiln:build` does not set it, because Rails picks the gem groups to load before the task starts, so pass `RAILS_ENV=production` for a site you deploy. The last line of the output names the environment. The output goes to `dist/`. A build writes into a temporary directory next to it and replaces `dist/` only when it succeeds, so a failed build leaves the previous output as it was. Add it to `.gitignore` unless you commit the output on purpose. A successful build prints the number of files written, the output path and, from the second build on, the pages added, removed and changed since the previous one:

```text
Kiln build succeeded: 42 files written.
Pages since last build: 1 added, 0 removed, 2 changed
  added: /blog/hello
  changed: /
  changed: /blog
Output: /path/to/app/dist (production, 3.1s)
```

A failed build prints every error, grouped by kind, and exits with status 1. Set `VERBOSE=1` to see the request log while it renders.

The comparison with the previous build reads `tmp/kiln-manifest.json`, which the build writes outside the output, so it is never published.

## Look at the output

```sh
bin/rails kiln:serve
```

`kiln:serve` serves the output the way Cloudflare Pages and GitHub Pages resolve paths: the exact file, then `x.html`, then `x/index.html`. It applies the exact rules in `_redirects` and answers unknown paths with `404.html`. It reads `PORT` (default 4000) and `BIND` (default `localhost`). Kiln adds no dev server for writing pages: keep using `bin/rails server`.

## Fresh checkouts

Propshaft only serves asset folders that exist when the app boots. Track `app/assets/builds/.keep`, as a new Rails app does. Without it, a build on a fresh checkout stops with one error that says so.
